**Read:** [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), chapter 6 in full; [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), microscopic heat capacity and carrier statistics.

Study the precise form of the third-law statement and the conditions under which entropy approaches a limiting value. Distinguish a perfect equilibrium crystal from residual configurational disorder, constrained states and a system that has not equilibrated on the measurement time scale.

Low-temperature heat capacities expose microscopic excitations and cannot generally be extrapolated as constant to zero temperature. Integrating C/T requires appropriate limiting behavior and treatment of transitions. Practical unattainability statements concern processes and resources, not merely replacing T by zero in an algebraic formula.

Read the complete cryogenic/third-law treatment, derive an entropy integral with a physically sensible low-temperature capacity and identify the measurement needed to test it. Refrigeration hardware, cryogenic supply and safe facility operation are deeper engineering subjects connected to this physical foundation.

